<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WW">Example <span data-tag="06WW">9.6.8.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category and let $S_ L$ and $S_ R$ be collections of morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then $(S_ L, S_ R)$ is a factorization system on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="06VS" href="/tag/06VS">9.6.0.1</a>) if and only if it is a factorization system on the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04PD" href="/tag/04PD">9.6.8.1</a>). The only nontrivial point is to verify that, in the latter case, the collections $S_ L$ and $S_ R$ are closed under retracts: see Corollary <a data-tag="04QF" href="/tag/04QF">9.6.8.10</a> below. </p>
